[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-4694"] :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-3)=0$ gives the solution $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3x+9=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =9-36 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-6913/selection/2a5ccdf4c5ff93070db5428c796ddf8a07da996ff5ecd4a5bcb616f8a02f59f8" :answer/id #uuid "4863b7d9-e542-59d6-9d18-6b7e5cb3485a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6913/selection/e5d103e2c07ae759d9eddce65c552ccfa6a5a00acf0aea2f18942b9a819f2577" :answer/id #uuid "fd852a2f-ac3c-5b80-9c7e-73a1924aef5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6913/selection/965aa5c98136d8e35adef10437bec7dcaa5ea245557ace16b44703e422ea6239" :answer/id #uuid "80f56930-3850-520c-9abd-ede0c6c067c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6913/selection/25de97e0cbf440101fa2f4e8740e4610708c595cc7aac47b53097fbc80ac64e1" :answer/id #uuid "9dfe1985-d4cb-5492-bd5d-9558e920c0b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6913/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" :answer/id #uuid "ea841a13-1cac-51d7-b05c-de5daabcbf75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6913/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "42e10a13-8285-506a-bade-53788ddaf33a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-6913/selection/e5d103e2c07ae759d9eddce65c552ccfa6a5a00acf0aea2f18942b9a819f2577" :answer-field/choices ["q-6913/selection/2a5ccdf4c5ff93070db5428c796ddf8a07da996ff5ecd4a5bcb616f8a02f59f8" "q-6913/selection/e5d103e2c07ae759d9eddce65c552ccfa6a5a00acf0aea2f18942b9a819f2577" "q-6913/selection/965aa5c98136d8e35adef10437bec7dcaa5ea245557ace16b44703e422ea6239" "q-6913/selection/25de97e0cbf440101fa2f4e8740e4610708c595cc7aac47b53097fbc80ac64e1" "q-6913/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-6913" :question/id #uuid "2d87d550-5004-5d22-b9a3-b2335e96082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6913" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-6913/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-6913"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-269990/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "5128d119-c06c-5daa-9e3b-d11bb41c4c21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-269990/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b494e80d-ad80-5ac0-93b8-45b7a1db27c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-269990/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-269990/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-269990" :question/id #uuid "4f76e5fd-4f42-5a2b-bb6e-329cbea38e79" :question/math-academy-id "q-269990" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+3}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-269990/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-269990"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-270004/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "68a605d8-5cbb-574b-aba6-af233cb8240e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-270004/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "0bf8a4b5-2e48-51ce-9fd7-b6e8f4b6c3e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-270004/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2dd1778a-d903-52e6-b178-078939c8c056" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-270004/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-270004/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-270004/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-270004" :question/id #uuid "9aa64d6e-83a2-5370-b8dd-754f74c2d8ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-270004" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2({x}^{3}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{2({x}^{3}-{1}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{2(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-1)=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-270004/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-270004"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-278535/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "18d954d3-7da1-55ef-aafc-3da0ef6c6fa1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278535/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbf5b17-be2f-5fbe-8a10-e2627bcddc0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278535/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-278535/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278535" :question/id #uuid "ee6e8ddb-5fef-526b-86e8-b1c6eaaae58c" :question/math-academy-id "q-278535" :question/is-example false :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+2)(\\cancel{x-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-278535/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-278535"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
